From fc98941a729038e5d280717bfd68799f1b541c5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aquarat <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:58:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] media: apple: avd: t8103: set VP_INSN_FIFO_MASK to 0

t8103_configure_stream() writes 0x100000 to AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_MASK
while the t8112/t8122 paths write 0. With 0x100000 the VP stalls in the
middle of large frames on T8103: no error IRQ is raised, the per-VP
"slice bytes parsed" counter stops short of the slice size (e.g.
303915 of 358341 bytes for a 2304x1296 Baseline IDR, 273060 of 290627
for a 1920x1080 CABAC IDR, 202426 of 218230 for a cam1 camera's
2304x1296 High IDR) and the 2 s watchdog fires ("Frame processing timed
out! Vp: 2 (00)"). Small or low-bitrate frames of the same geometry
decode fine, which is why this looked like a buffer sizing problem.

Writing 0, like the other variants do, makes every such frame decode
bit-identically to software.

Backport of asahi-7.1.12-1 commit ca9a850f237f ("fixup! media: apple:
add avd driver") by Janne Grunau to asahi-7.1.6-1, now with the
observed effect on T8103 documented.

Tested on Apple M1 (T8103): 1920x1080 Main/High (CABAC, B-frames),
2304x1296 Baseline/Main/High at 2-4 Mbit/s including a real cam1
camera stream (150 frames, PPS scaling lists) all go from watchdog
timeout on the first frame to bit-identical output; the whole
26-clip matrix is bit-identical. Also verified with only this patch on
top of the two H.264 fixes and with all other experiments disabled.

Signed-off-by: Aquarat <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
---
 avd-hw.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hw.c b/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hw.c
index 168d68c..a3987ac 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hw.c
@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@ void t8103_configure_stream(struct avd_dev *avd, dma_addr_t addr, u8 fifo_idx,
 			  u32 vp_slot)
 {
 	w32(AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_IOVA + (fifo_idx * 4), addr >> 8);
-	w32(AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_MASK + (fifo_idx * 4), 0x100000);
+	w32(AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_MASK + (fifo_idx * 4), 0);
 	w32(AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_CACH + (fifo_idx * 4), 0);
 	w32(AVD_V3_VP_INSN_FIFO_XFER + (fifo_idx * 4), 0);
 
